// Practice quiz: Two pointers: reversing and counting
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so loop conditions are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/two-pointers-reversing-and-counting

::o1 why a char array::Why does a two-index reversal copy the String into a char array with toCharArray() first? {
~toCharArray() already returns the characters in reverse order#Not quite. It copies them in their original order; the loop does the reversing.
~A String has no way to read a single character#Not quite. charAt reads one character; the problem is that a String cannot be changed.
=A String cannot be changed, but a char array can, so the swaps happen in the array and new String(chars) builds the result#Correct. Strings are constant, so the reversed text must be built as a new String.
~char arrays are always faster, but a String would also work#Not quite. A String cannot be changed in place, so swapping its characters is impossible.
}

::o1 which pairs swap::A reversal of "abcd" puts i on the first index and j on the last and swaps until they cross. Which index pairs are swapped? {
=0 with 3, then 1 with 2#Correct. After 1 and 2 swap, i becomes 2 and j becomes 1, so they have crossed and the loop stops.
~0 with 3, 1 with 2, 2 with 1, then 3 with 0#Not quite. Swapping each pair a second time would put the characters back where they started.
~Only 0 with 3#Not quite. The middle pair, 1 and 2, must be swapped too.
~0 with 1, then 2 with 3#Not quite. A reversal swaps mirrored positions, one from each end.
}

::o2 counting mirrored pairs::Two indexes count the mirrored pairs whose characters are equal, stopping when the indexes meet or cross. What count do they give for "noon"? {
~4#Not quite. The loop counts pairs, not single characters, and "noon" has two mirrored pairs.
=2#Correct. Index 0 and 3 are both n, and index 1 and 2 are both o; then the indexes cross.
~1#Not quite. Both mirrored pairs match, not just the outer one.
~0#Not quite. Each mirrored pair in "noon" holds two equal characters.
}

::o2 one loop instead of two::Why use two indexes instead of nested loops to count mirrored pairs? {
~Nested loops cannot read characters from a String#Not quite. They can; they just do much more work than this job needs.
~Two indexes compare every character with every other character#Not quite. They compare only each character with its mirror.
~There is no difference in the work done#Not quite. Nested loops grow with the square of the length; one inward loop does not.
=One loop visits each mirrored pair once, while nested loops compare every character with every other one#Correct. Nested loops do far more work and also compare characters that are not mirrors of each other.
}

::o3 meeting in the middle::The pair counter for "radar" uses the condition i at most j instead of i less than j. What goes wrong? {
=The middle character is paired with itself and counted, giving 3 instead of 2#Correct. When i and j both reach the middle, it equals itself, so an extra pair is counted.
~Nothing changes for an odd length#Not quite. With an odd length the indexes meet on the middle character, and the extra pass counts it.
~The loop never stops#Not quite. The indexes still cross one pass later, so the loop ends.
~It throws an exception for reading past the end#Not quite. Both indexes stay inside the word; the problem is the extra middle pass.
}

::o3 choosing the condition::Which loop condition stops two inward-moving indexes exactly when they meet or cross, for both odd and even lengths? {
~while i is not equal to j#Not quite. For an even length the indexes cross without ever being equal, so the loop runs past the middle.
~while i is less than the word's length#Not quite. That walks i over the whole word and handles every pair twice.
~while i is at most j#Not quite. For an odd length that adds a pass where the middle character meets itself.
=while i is less than j#Correct. It is false as soon as the indexes are equal or have crossed, so each pair is handled once.
}
